# Run artifacts are large, machine-specific and reproducible from the tools. 701 MB of them, and
# 846 JSON files. They are evidence for a verdict, not source: drop the contents.
*
!.gitignore

# EXCEPT the two kinds that are neither large nor reproducible.
#
# 1. The written reports. A verdict cites `reports/<piece>-survey.md` as its evidence, and until
#    now that path was unversioned — so an agent that overwrote one had destroyed the thing a
#    verdict rests on with no history to recover it from. That happened: the W1-05 builder
#    overwrote its own journey artifact, restored what it could exactly, and had to flag two
#    fields it could not recover rather than invent them. 106 files, 800 KB.
# 2. The blog feed. It is the owner's view of progress and it is append-only by convention, which
#    is exactly the file a careless `>` destroys.
# git cannot re-include a file inside an excluded DIRECTORY, so descend first, then pick.
!*/
!**/*.md
!blog-feed.jsonl

# 3. BASELINES. Corrected by the W1-MASS builder, which caught the reasoning above being wrong for
#    exactly one class of file: "that test is right for a measurement and exactly wrong for a
#    baseline, whose whole value is being the OLD value." A run artifact can be regenerated
#    because you want today's number. A baseline exists to be yesterday's, so regenerating it
#    destroys the only thing it was for. The render-probe baseline had already rotted — all three
#    of its pixel-exact hashes differ from HEAD, changed eight hours earlier by another piece —
#    with nothing going red, because no gate re-runs it and git was not holding the old value.
!**/*baseline*.json
!**/*-fingerprint*.json

# 4. THE SABOTAGE REPLAY CORPUS, by the same argument, pushed one step further. The nine
#    artifacts `tools/experience/sabotage.mjs --cases` replays are not merely files you would
#    rather not regenerate — SEVERAL OF THEM CANNOT BE REGENERATED AT ALL. §0_goes_red of the
#    W1-04 collision report is the PRE-FIX `__w1_04_townSolids`; the fix landed and that verb
#    does not exist at HEAD. Two of the souls artifacts are browser runs with guards deleted from
#    `sim/souls.js` and `engine.js`; both guards are present at HEAD. The test above — "is this
#    reproducible by re-running the tool that made it" — answers NO, so by the file's own rule
#    they belong in history. Excluding them meant a fresh clone reported ABSENT and exited 8
#    (W1-25-r1 §C): honest, and useless.
#    They live as byte-for-byte tracked twins under reports/experience/sabotage-corpus/, written
#    and hash-verified by `tools/experience/sabotage-corpus.mjs`. 379 KB, nine files, plus a
#    SHA-256 manifest so a twin that drifts from an origin still on the container is caught.
!**/sabotage-case-*.json

# Still ignored, deliberately: reports/runs/ raw logs (tools/run.mjs writes there by design),
# every .json artifact, and every capture. Those are reproducible by re-running the tool that
# made them, which is the test for whether something belongs in history.
reports/runs/
